{{ macros.section(2, "Self Extracting Executables", id="self-extracting-executables") }}

One of the strangest features of `compose.mk` is that it can **create self-extracting executable archives** from Makefiles (or anything else really) using a dockerized version of [makeself](https://makeself.io/).[^makeself]

{{macros.img_link("escher.jpg", mkdocs, "78%",class=" ")}}

Besides offering [generic access to makeself](#makeself-reference), `compose.mk` offers simple support for bundling a project Makefile, plus any dependencies, **into a single executable file.**  Dependencies might include things like `compose.mk` itself, data files, and any docker compose file you're using.

!!! terminology "Terminology"
    This technique is more like packaging or bundling than compilation, because there's no way to remove the dependency on `make`, and we still need an interpreter after decompressing archives. This caveat is often safe to ignore though since `make` is nearly as ubiquitous as shell.
    
    For a simpler approach to packaging that doesn't require archives, see instead the documentation for [guests and payloads]({{_}}/standard-lib#forks-guests-and-payloads).
    
    For the separate topic of transpiling cmk-lang to Makefile, see instead the [compiler docs]({{_}}/cmk/compiler).

Packaging can remove the explicit dependency on the rest of the stack represented by your project filesystem state though, **resulting in frozen 'release' where you can set a custom entrypoint.**  

### Use-Cases & Motivation
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

There are two main reasons why native support for this is interesting:

* First, `compose.mk` is both a [tool factory]({{_}}/demos/custom-automation-apis/) and [an application factory]({{_}}/embedded-tui), and wanting to "package" your way into a frozen release comes up naturally.
* Second, *project automation and pipelines* are also a big part of what `compose.mk` is used for, and compressing *that* down into a snapshotted "release" could be useful for stuff like *checkpointing/rollbacks*, or for topics in notebookes / reproducible research.  See also the [notebooking demo]({{_}}/demos/notebooking) for a sketch of something like that.

!!! question "Hmm.."
    More philosophically: the ability to reference, run, and script over potentially *lots* of possibly customized containers in an organized way using `docker-compose.yml` and `compose.mk` without *explicitly shipping either* feels like a different way to build and release tools.
    
Put simply, [easy access to containers]({{_}}/container-dispatch) [and multiple programming languages]({{_}}/demos/polyglots) and [workflows]({{_}}/demos/workflows) is usually stuff that we associate with microservices and distributed systems like Kubernetes, Jenkins or Airflow.  What happens if it is easy to design *undistributed* applications, tools, and pipelines with similar architectures?  

Another point of view is that what we're really talking about is **the ability to export a project into a tool**.  Because let's be real: *No one wants to look at your project, because it's a mess of docs/examples/tests/support data, and they have to clone it, then understand how it's all organized before they can even try to use it.  Usually, even after a project is understood, it's not like you can *use* it directly, it must adapted or edited, or you go down a rabbit hole of dependencies.*  Self-extracting archives are far from *new*, but since they can actually help with some of this, they seem underutilized.

### Example: Make-target to Executable 
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

Shortcuts for packaging up targets-as-entrypoints use [`lang.pkg`]({{_}}/api#mkpkg).

For the first example, we'll spin-off an individual utility from `compose.mk` as a stand-alone executable.  Let's look at the {{site.api_link('stream.img', mkdocs)}} target, which uses a dockerized version of the `chafa` tool for displaying images on console, comparable to `imgcat` or similar.  It takes no arguments, and works with streams.

```bash {.cli_example}
$ bin=./stream.img ./compose.mk lang.pkg/stream.img
$ cat img.png | ./stream.img
```

It works, and we use `chafa` without *having* chafa, because containers don't build or pull before they are used.

### Example: TUI to Executable
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

While `stream.img` is just a small utility, the approach demonstrated above also works with "applications" implemented using `compose.mk`, i.e. [full blown TUIs]({{_}}/embedded-tui).  

As an example, how about exporting the {{site.api_link('tux.demo',mkdocs)}}?

```bash {.cli_example}
$ bin=./tux.demo ./compose.mk lang.pkg/tux.demo
```

### Example: Extension to Executable
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

In the examples so far, the `./compose.mk lang.pkg/<target>` examples only use *targets that are built-in*.  If you want to extend `compose.mk` with your own automation and export *that*, then you'll want to use `lang.pkg` from the extension instead.

As an example, let's export the tools built as part of the [Custom Automation APIs Demo]({{_}}/demos/custom-automation-apis).

```bash {.cli_example}
# create binary for the `ansible.adhoc.pipe` target
$ bin=./ansible.adhoc archive=demos \
    ./demos/ansible-adhoc.mk lang.pkg/ansible.adhoc.pipe

# confirm/inspect the generated executable
$ file ./ansible.adhoc 
```
```raw {.cli_output}
./ansible.adhoc: POSIX shell script executable (binary data)
```

```bash {.cli_example}
# use ansible without having ansible
$ echo msg=HelloWorld | module=debug ./ansible.adhoc
```
```json {.cli_output}
{
  "action": "debug",
  "changed": false,
  "msg": "HelloWorld"
}
```

### Example: CMK App to Executable
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

For freezing a [cmk-lang]({{_}}/cmk/compiler) program (or a `.mk` makefile), the friendly subcommand **`cmk build <file>`** does it in one step: it picks a sensible `bin` name from the file and delegates to `lang.pkg` under the hood (no `archive=` juggling, no manual `cmk run`).  `lang.pkg` remains the general form; it also packages a *target-as-entrypoint* (the examples above); `cmk build` is just the file-oriented shortcut.  Take this small structured-IO app, which emits a key/value and consumes it back:

```Makefile {.snippet .language-Makefile}
emit:
	🡄 key=val

consume:
	🡆 .key

__main__:
	this.emit | this.consume
	cmk.log(hello world!)
```

```bash {.cli_example}
# freeze the .cmk (bin defaults to the file's basename)
$ ./compose.mk cmk build demos/cmk/structured-io.cmk

# ...equivalent to the underlying packaging target:
$ ./compose.mk lang.pkg/demos/cmk/structured-io.cmk

# now portable, runs anywhere, with no compose.mk / source on disk:
$ ./structured-io
```
```bash {.cli_output}
val
⇄ __main__ // hello world!
```

Under the hood this bundles `compose.mk` plus your `.cmk` and sets the entrypoint to re-interpret the bundled source, so the freed binary **transpiles the CMK on each run** (you'll see the compiler's progress before your output).  Passing a `.mk` file instead works the same way, with the makefile's default goal as the entrypoint.

!!! tip "Bundling dependencies"
    A self-contained `.cmk`/`.mk` needs nothing extra.  If yours pulls in sibling files (a relative `include`, or `import.*` of another file), add them to the archive with `archive=` (it passes through `cmk build` to `lang.pkg`), e.g. `archive=demos ./compose.mk cmk build demos/cmk/underload.cmk`.

### Example: Omnibus Distribution
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

An app that resolves a plugin from `CMK_PLUGINS_DIR` (the project-local `.cmk` directory by default) breaks once that directory is gone from the host.  The **`cmk omni`** subcommand handles this: it packages exactly like `cmk build`, then also bundles the host context's `CMK_PLUGINS_DIR` and `CMK_MODULES_DIR` directories into the archive, so the frozen binary carries its plugins and modules along with it.

```bash {.cli_example}
# freeze an app plus the plugins it resolves from ./.cmk
$ ./compose.mk cmk omni demos/cmk/strat.cmk

# with no file, freeze the compose.mk root plus plugins
$ ./compose.mk cmk omni
```

The output defaults to `./omnibus` (override with `bin=<name>`).  Bundled directories land at the archive root by basename, and since the archive runs from its own extraction directory, the default `.cmk` layout resolves automatically with no extra setup.  A search path pointing at absolute or differently-named directories is still bundled, but needs `CMK_PLUGINS_DIR` set at runtime to be found.

### Makeself Reference
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">

Direct access to `makeself` is via {{site.api_link('mk.self',mkdocs)}}.  For those unfamiliar with `makeself`, this section is a quick tutorial, but you can use this to package up anything.  

Let's look at an example of freezing the contents of a directory and an arbitrary script that *uses* that directory.  

We only need to provide a space-separated list of stuff to archive, plus a name for the executable file, plus a script for the entrypoint. The script can be anything as long as it works properly relative to the archive root.. so for now we'll just run `pwd` followed by `find .` to demonstrate that we are indeed running *in* the archive.

```bash {.cli_example}
$ archive=docs/demos bin=archive.run \
  script='sh -c "pwd; find ."' ./compose.mk mk.self
```
```bash {.cli_output}
≣ docker.from.def // Dockerfile.makeself 
 ≣ docker.from.def // tag=compose.mk:makeself .. cached 
⇄ mk.self // Archive for docs/demos will be released as ./archive.run
⇄ mk.self // Total files: 13
⇄ mk.self // Entrypoint: sh -c "pwd; find ."
Header is 716 lines long
About to compress 100 KB of data...
Adding files to archive named "archive.run"...
./demos/README.md
CRC: ...
MD5: ...
Self-extractable archive "archive.run" successfully created.
```

So far so good.  Running the exported `archive.run` bin looks like this:

```bash {.cli_example}
$  ./archive.run 
```
```bash {.cli_output}
/home/user/code/.tmp.oZWg1AYyc
./demos
./demos/README.md
```

### Argument Passing 
<hr class="section-rule lvl-3">
Argument passing with makeself hasn't been demonstrated so far, and this can get tricky.  Here's an example where the generated executable we want to build will *take arguments in the form of a shell script*, which script will run inside the archive directory.  In this case since we're passing in a script, we need to quote all the input like this:
  
```bash {.cli_example}
$ archive=demos/ bin=archive.run script='bash -x -c' ./compose.mk mk.self 
$ ./archive.run -- 'pwd; find .'

```
```bash {.cli_output}

+ pwd
/home/user/.tmp.5idV3NHp6
+ find .
./demos
./demos/README.md
``` 

!!! danger "Limitation"
    A major limitation of `makeself` is that *passing arguments to the generated executable always requires the ` -- ` syntax that you see above.*  

To pass arguments to `makeself` you can put stuff before the ` -- `.  Mostly this isn't useful because `compose.mk` already uses `makeself` in such a way that `--quiet --noprogress` is implied for pipe-safety.  For debugging workflows though, check out `--keep` to preserve the uncompressed archive, and `--noexec` to disable the script execution.  See also the output of `./archive.run --help`, which provides help for `makeself` and **not** for whatever you've packaged with it.

